From: shanshan <samsom0409@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Oct 2026 02:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] take the row count from the tiling when one chunk holds every row

`InitWorkSpaceSplitConfig` recovers M by summing the whole group list on every
core. The tiling already carries that number: the host reads it from the
activation's first dimension (base_tiling.cpp:201) and stores it (:434), and
postProcess sizes the output scale buffer by it (post.h:106).

The substitution is not free of assumptions and should not be read as such.
It requires `sum(group_list)` and the tiling's M to agree. They are already
required to agree: with the activation indexed and the output scale buffer
sized by the tiling's M while the chunking is derived from the table, feeding
the operator a group list whose sum differs from the row count is accepted
without complaint and produces a wrong answer today. So the change picks which
of two quantities that must already match to read, rather than introducing a
new way to be wrong. It is gated on the one-chunk case so every other loop
count keeps deriving M from the table exactly as before, and it is a separate
commit so it can be dropped on its own.

Measured on one Ascend910_93 die, A8W4 MSD, K=6144 N=256, 8 active experts of
256 each holding one row, applied on top of the straight-line path and the
closed-form chunk span. Both forms were built into one binary selected at run
time, so the pair shares a process, a device context, one HBM allocation and
one set of weight banks; six 192.75 MiB banks rotate with the active expert
window so each batch of 32 calls touches 202.1 MB of distinct weight addresses
against a 192 MiB L2. The batch is captured into an ACL graph and replayed,
because eager calls to this operator cost as much on the host as the kernel
costs on the device. Three runs of 40 rounds, arms replayed A,B,B,A per round:

  sum the table    89.148   88.853   90.255 us per call
  tiling's M       85.211   88.208   88.157
  saved             3.882    0.663    2.127   (sd 0.207, 1.530, 0.264)

The first and third runs are 0 positive against 40 negative; the second has a
standard deviation an order of magnitude larger than the other two and a mixed
sign count, and a neighbouring tenant was active on the die, so read 2 to 4 us
rather than the mean of the three.

One caveat on what this does not buy. Most of what the group list costs is not
any single traversal but the first one: with this operator's four readers of
the table, eliding one hands the cost to the next. Cutting the kernel entry
just after `op.Init` prices that Init at 38.55 us with a 256-slot table and at
zero with an 8-slot one, and eliding the two pipeline-level traversals moves
the same 38 us out of Init and into `Process` while leaving the call's total
unchanged at 88 to 93 us. What this commit removes is one traversal's worth of
warm-cache reads, not that first-traversal cost.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/csrc/gmm/grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2/op_kernel/grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2_a8w4_msd_pipeline.h
+++ b/csrc/gmm/grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2/op_kernel/grouped_matmul_swiglu_quant_v2_a8w4_msd_pipeline.h
@@ -170,6 +170,15 @@
 {
     if (gmmSwigluQuantV2BaseParams->groupListType == 0) {
         workspaceSplitConfig.M = groupListGM.GetValue(gmmSwigluQuantV2->groupListLen - 1);
+    } else if (Ceil(gmmSwigluQuantV2BaseParams->M, gmmSwigluQuantV2BaseParams->mLimit) == 1) {
+        // One chunk holds every row, so take the count from the tiling instead
+        // of summing the table. The two quantities must already agree: the host
+        // takes the tiling's M from the activation's first dimension
+        // (base_tiling.cpp:201), postProcess sizes the output scale buffer by it
+        // (post.h:106), and the chunking is derived from the table, so a
+        // disagreement is a wrong answer today rather than a new failure mode.
+        // Every other loop count still sums the table.
+        workspaceSplitConfig.M = gmmSwigluQuantV2BaseParams->M;
     } else {
         int64_t totalTmp = 0;
         for (uint32_t i = 0; i < gmmSwigluQuantV2->groupListLen; i++) {
